"""`aspect worktree` — git worktrees that keep their Bazel server warm.

`git worktree add` at a fresh path gives Bazel a workspace it has never seen:
new server, loading and analysis from zero, `external/` rebuilt. These tasks
hand out a worktree at a **reused** path instead, so Bazel's own derivation of
the output base lands on the same directory as last time. The `external/` tree
and action cache come back unconditionally; the running server and its analysis
cache come back too when the build's flags match, since Bazel discards analysis
on a configuration change. No flag is injected to arrange any of it.

    aspect worktree add fix/login
    slot=$(aspect worktree path fix/login) && cd "$slot"
    bazel test //...                   ->  the slot's output base, and its
                                           server too if the flags match
    aspect worktree remove fix/login   ->  the slot goes back to the pool

Nothing inside the slot has to be an Aspect command: Bazel derives the base
from the workspace directory, so anything invoking Bazel there gets the same
state.

A slot is reached by its own path — nothing is symlinked into place. `git
worktree list` already reports that path, so a worktree stays legible to git
and to anyone who falls back to managing it by hand, with nothing outside git
left to dangle.

Skipping `remove` costs nothing in correctness: git is the authority on whether
a worktree exists, so a slot whose worktree git has forgotten is free whatever
the registry says.

An agent that dies with its checkout still in place is the harder case, because
git still knows about that worktree. The lease is taken back only when the
session behind it is positively gone and the checkout is clean — a harness that
merely stopped running is usually a closed terminal, and `list` offers the
command to resume it rather than offering the slot to someone else.

Leases record who took them, so `list` names the session holding each slot.
Detection is automatic for harnesses the CLI recognizes, and
`ASPECT_AGENT_KIND` / `ASPECT_AGENT_ID` or `--agent-kind` / `--agent-id` cover
the rest.

Every task that writes the registry does so under the pool lock, and leaves it
consistent with git before returning. `add` holds that lock only to pick a slot
and to record the result: the checkout in between runs with the lock released,
against a slot reserved in this caller's name, so agents starting together are
not queued behind each other's `git worktree add`. See
`private/lib/worktree.axl` for the pool model, why slots have fixed paths, and
how the pool is bounded.
"""

load("@std//time.axl", "time")
load(
    "./private/lib/agent.axl",
    "GONE",
    "RESUMABLE",
    "RUNNING",
    "detect",
    "label",
    "resume_hint",
    "session_state",
)
load("./private/lib/confirm.axl", "confirm")
load("./private/lib/environment.axl", "info", "warn")
load("./private/lib/output_bases.axl", "output_bases")
load("./private/lib/table.axl", "Column", "legend", "render")
load(
    "./private/lib/worktree.axl",
    "FREE",
    "LEASED",
    "PRUNE_AGE_DAYS",
    "RESERVED",
    "abandoned_lease",
    "acquire",
    "close_lease",
    "default_score",
    "drop_slot",
    "duration_phrase",
    "free_slot",
    "held_for_ms",
    "holds_reservation",
    "leased_slot",
    "lock_digest",
    "lock_digest_at",
    "new_slot_id",
    "new_slot_record",
    "output_base_of",
    "owned_slots",
    "pool_of",
    "prunable_slots",
    "read_registry",
    "reconcile",
    "record_handover",
    "record_lease",
    "release",
    "reservation_abandoned",
    "reserve_slot",
    "reserving_slot",
    "run_git",
    "slot_age_days",
    "slot_created_days",
    "slot_path",
    "slug",
    "uncommitted_work",
    "warmth_of",
    "write_registry",
)
load("./private/lib/worktrees_trait.axl", "Worktrees")

# --- constants ---------------------------------------------------------------

_POOL_BUSY = "another `aspect worktree` call is holding this pool's lock; try again"

# Contention is not failure: the caller's request was well-formed and will
# succeed shortly. Agents allocate in parallel and need to tell "retry in a
# moment" from "this is broken", which one exit code for both cannot express.
# 75 is sysexits.h EX_TEMPFAIL, which is what it means.
_EX_TEMPFAIL = 75

_SCHEMA_VERSION = 1

# --- refusals ----------------------------------------------------------------

def _refuse(ctx: TaskContext, code: str, message: str, exit_code: int = 1, retryable: bool = False) -> TaskConclusion:
    """A refusal in whichever form the caller asked for.

    Every one of these is something an agent has to branch on, and a
    `--output=json` consumer handed prose on stderr and an empty stdout would
    have to parse English to do it. `code` is the stable token to branch on.
    """
    if ctx.args.output == "json":
        ctx.std.io.stdout.write(json.encode({
            "schema_version": _SCHEMA_VERSION,
            "error": code,
            "retryable": retryable,
            "message": message,
        }, indent = 2) + "\n")
        return TaskConclusion(exit_code = exit_code)
    return TaskConclusion(exit_code = exit_code, message = message)

def _pool_busy(ctx: TaskContext) -> TaskConclusion:
    """The refusal when another call holds the pool lock."""
    return _refuse(ctx, "pool_busy", _POOL_BUSY, exit_code = _EX_TEMPFAIL, retryable = True)

def _branch_pending(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, slot_id: str, name: str) -> TaskConclusion:
    """The refusal when another `add` is checking this branch out right now.

    Retryable, like `pool_busy` and unlike `branch_in_use`: the other call is
    seconds from finishing, and what the caller finds then — a lease it can take
    over, or its own slot — is a different answer from this one.
    """
    return _refuse(
        ctx,
        "branch_pending",
        "another `aspect worktree add` is checking " + name + " out at " +
        slot_path(pool, slot_id) + " right now; try again in a moment",
        exit_code = _EX_TEMPFAIL,
        retryable = True,
    )

# --- git ---------------------------------------------------------------------

def _git(ctx: TaskContext, args: list[str], cwd: str = "") -> str:
    """`git <args>` stdout. Fails the task when git does — for calls whose
    failure means the operation cannot proceed."""
    r = run_git(ctx.std, args, cwd)
    if not r.ok:
        fail("git " + " ".join(args) + " failed: " + r.err)
    return r.out

def _git_try(ctx: TaskContext, args: list[str], cwd: str = "") -> str:
    """`git <args>` stdout, or "" when git exits non-zero — for probes where a
    failure is itself the answer, such as asking whether a branch exists."""
    r = run_git(ctx.std, args, cwd)
    return r.out if r.ok else ""

# --- entering the pool -------------------------------------------------------

def _output_root(ctx: TaskContext) -> str:
    """The output user root Bazel would use here, resolved as `aspect gc` and
    `aspect output-bases` resolve it: the rc chain, else the platform default.

    Resolved once per command and handed to everything that derives a slot's
    output base. A repo with `startup --output_user_root=` in its `.bazelrc`
    keeps its bases elsewhere, and a pool looking in the platform default finds
    nothing there — every slot reads cold, and prune retires warm slots while
    leaving the real bases behind.
    """
    return output_bases.output_user_root(ctx, "")

def _pool(ctx: TaskContext) -> dict:
    """The pool for the working directory, or an empty dict outside a clone.

    Empty rather than fatal: being in the wrong directory is an ordinary
    mistake for an agent to make, and it deserves the same typed refusal as
    every other, not a traceback.
    """
    return pool_of(ctx.std, ctx.std.env.current_dir())

def _not_a_repository(ctx: TaskContext) -> TaskConclusion:
    return _refuse(
        ctx,
        "not_a_repository",
        "not inside a git repository — run this from a clone of the repo you want a worktree of",
    )

def _lock_pool(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict) -> dict:
    """Hold the pool's lock for the rest of the task, or {} if another caller
    has it.

    Only the tasks that write the registry take it. The release is deferred
    rather than written out at each exit: `ctx.defer` runs even when the task
    dies on a fatal error, which is the only way to guarantee the lock is
    dropped rather than left for the next caller to reclaim.

    The handle is mutable because `add` gives the lock back for its checkout and
    takes it again afterwards. The deferred release reads it at the end and so
    drops whichever lock is held — or nothing, if the task ended while unlocked.
    """
    token = acquire(ctx.std, pool["dir"])
    if not token:
        return {}
    held = {"token": token}
    ctx.defer(lambda: release(ctx.std, pool["dir"], held["token"]))
    return held

def _unlock(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, held: dict) -> None:
    """Give the lock back, for work that must not be done holding it."""
    release(ctx.std, pool["dir"], held["token"])
    held["token"] = ""

def _relock(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, held: dict) -> bool:
    """Take the lock again. False when another caller has it."""
    held["token"] = acquire(ctx.std, pool["dir"])
    return held["token"] != ""

# --- choosing a slot ---------------------------------------------------------

def _score_slot(ctx: TaskContext, slot: dict, want: dict) -> int:
    """The repo's scoring hook if one is registered, else the built-in.

    Allocation has no correctness consequence — a worse pick costs a colder
    build — which is what makes it safe to hand to a config.axl hook.
    """
    for hook in ctx.traits[Worktrees].score_slot:
        return hook(ctx, slot, want)
    return default_score(slot, want)

def _worktree_holding_branch(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, name: str) -> str:
    """The path of any worktree of this clone that has `name` checked out, or "".

    Covers what the registry cannot see: worktrees created by hand, and the
    clone's own main checkout. Git refuses to put one branch in two worktrees,
    and without this the refusal arrives from inside `_materialize`, after the
    chosen slot's previous occupant has already been force-removed — warm state
    destroyed to no purpose, and an error naming a slot the caller never asked
    about.
    """
    porcelain = _git_try(ctx, ["worktree", "list", "--porcelain"], pool["git_common_dir"])
    at = ""
    for line in porcelain.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("worktree "):
            at = line[len("worktree "):].strip()
        elif line.strip() == "branch refs/heads/" + name:
            return at
    return ""

def _no_such_worktree(ctx: TaskContext, name: str) -> TaskConclusion:
    return _refuse(
        ctx,
        "no_such_worktree",
        "no worktree named " + name + " in this pool — `aspect worktree list` shows what is leased",
    )

def _dirty_files(ctx: TaskContext, path: str, root: str) -> str:
    """The uncommitted work in a slot, indented for a refusal, or "".

    Reported rather than counted: a caller deciding whether to discard work
    needs to see which files, not a number. Bazel's own leavings are not work
    and `uncommitted_work` drops them.
    """
    work = uncommitted_work(ctx.std, path, output_base_of(ctx.std, path, root))
    return "\n".join(["  " + line for line in work])

def _held_by(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, registry: dict, slot_id: str, name: str) -> str:
    """Why `name` cannot be taken, and what the caller can do instead.

    The lease knows which session holds it and whether that session is running,
    suspended or deleted. A refusal that reports only the slot path makes the
    caller go and find that out; one that reports it turns a dead end into a
    choice between resuming the other session and taking the slot.
    """
    lease = registry["slots"][slot_id].get("lease") or {}
    agent = lease.get("agent") or {}
    where = slot_path(pool, slot_id)
    if not agent:
        return (name + " is already checked out at " + where +
                ". Use it, or `aspect worktree remove " + name + "` first.")

    state = session_state(ctx.std, agent)
    who = label(agent)
    if state == RUNNING:
        held = name + " is checked out at " + where + ", held by a running " + who + " session."
    elif state == RESUMABLE:
        held = name + " is checked out at " + where + ", held by " + who + ", which is not running."
    else:
        held = name + " is checked out at " + where + ", held by " + who + "."

    lines = [held]
    hint = resume_hint(agent)
    if hint and state == RESUMABLE:
        lines.append("Resume that session: " + hint)
    lines.append("Or claim the slot for this session, keeping the checkout as it is: " +
                 "`aspect worktree add " + name + " --take-over`.")
    if state == RUNNING:
        lines.append("That session is still running, so check with whoever is driving it first.")
    return " ".join(lines)

def _take_over(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, registry: dict, slot_id: str, name: str) -> None:
    """Re-attribute a lease to this caller, leaving the checkout untouched.

    The slot already holds this branch, so nothing is recreated: no
    `worktree remove`, no re-checkout, and whatever the previous session left
    uncommitted is still there afterwards. The only thing that changes is who
    the lease says is responsible for it.
    """
    lease = registry["slots"][slot_id].get("lease") or {}
    previous = label(lease.get("agent") or {}) or "an unidentified session"
    warn(ctx.std, "taking " + name + " over from " + previous +
                  "; the checkout is untouched and anything uncommitted in it is now yours")
    agent = detect(ctx.std, ctx.args.agent_kind, ctx.args.agent_id)
    record_handover(registry, slot_id, name, agent)
    registry["slots"][slot_id]["lease"] = {
        "name": name,
        "since": time.now_ms(),
        "agent": agent,
    }

def _age_out(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, registry: dict, root: str) -> int:
    """Drop free slots nothing has touched in `PRUNE_AGE_DAYS`. Returns how many
    went, so the caller can persist a registry that no longer matches the disk.

    This is what actually bounds the pool, and it runs inside `add` because
    nobody runs a cleanup command on a schedule. It never touches a leased slot
    or one a live server still holds, so the worst case is reclaiming cache that
    had already stopped paying for itself.
    """
    dropped = 0
    for slot_id in prunable_slots(ctx.std, pool, registry, PRUNE_AGE_DAYS, root):
        label = registry["slots"][slot_id].get("last_branch", "") or slot_id
        age = slot_age_days(ctx.std, pool, slot_id, registry["slots"][slot_id], root)
        if drop_slot(ctx.std, pool, registry, slot_id, root).dropped:
            info(ctx.std, "pruned slot last used %d days ago (%s)" % (age, label))
            dropped += 1
    return dropped

def _reclaim_reservations(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, registry: dict) -> int:
    """Free slots an interrupted `add` was setting up. Returns how many went, so
    the caller can persist a registry that no longer matches the disk.

    A reservation is honoured only while the process behind it is alive, and
    this is the one place that judges it, because acting on the answer means
    writing the registry. Running it before allocation matters twice over: the
    slot is otherwise unallocatable for good, and the half-made worktree left in
    git would make the next request for that branch look as though it were
    checked out somewhere the pool does not manage.
    """
    freed = 0
    for slot_id in registry["slots"]:
        slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
        if slot["state"] != RESERVED or slot.get("owner_git_dir", "") != pool["git_common_dir"]:
            continue
        if not reservation_abandoned(ctx.std, registry, slot_id):
            continue
        pending = (slot.get("reservation") or {}).get("name", "")
        info(ctx.std, "reclaimed the slot an interrupted `aspect worktree add` was setting up" +
                      ((" for " + pending) if pending else ""))
        free_slot(ctx.std, pool, registry, slot_id)
        freed += 1
    return freed

def _drop_reservation(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, registry: dict, slot_id: str, token: str) -> None:
    """Give a reserved slot back after its checkout failed. Mutates `registry`.

    The slot returns to free with whatever it was last built for still recorded:
    that describes its output base, which this attempt never changed, so it is
    still what the next allocation should score it on.
    """
    if not holds_reservation(registry, slot_id, token):
        return
    catch(run_git, ctx.std, ["worktree", "remove", "--force", slot_path(pool, slot_id)], pool["git_common_dir"])
    registry["slots"][slot_id]["state"] = FREE
    registry["slots"][slot_id]["reservation"] = None

def _pick_slot(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, registry: dict, want: dict, root: str) -> struct:
    """The best free slot this clone owns for `want`, creating one if none fits.

    A slot is only a candidate when it belongs to this clone's object store:
    `git worktree add` cannot hand a worktree to a different one.

    Any free slot beats creating one, however badly it scores: a bad reuse pays
    the same `external/` refetch a new slot would and skips the new JVM, the
    from-zero analysis and one more output base on disk. A `score_slot` hook may
    still veto with 0, and then a new slot is the only way to serve the caller —
    but a hook that vetoes everything would otherwise grow the pool without
    bound, so past `max_slots` the veto yields to the best available slot.
    """
    best = ""
    best_score = 0
    for slot_id in registry["slots"]:
        slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
        if slot["state"] != FREE or slot.get("owner_git_dir", "") != pool["git_common_dir"]:
            continue
        score = _score_slot(ctx, slot, want)
        if score > best_score:
            best = slot_id
            best_score = score

    if best:
        return struct(id = best, score = best_score, reused_from = registry["slots"][best].get("last_branch", ""))

    # Before growing, look for a lease whose session no longer exists. This is
    # checked here rather than in `reconcile` because it costs a `git status`
    # per candidate, and the only moment the answer changes anything is the one
    # where the alternative is another slot on disk.
    for slot_id in registry["slots"]:
        if registry["slots"][slot_id]["state"] != LEASED:
            continue
        if registry["slots"][slot_id].get("owner_git_dir", "") != pool["git_common_dir"]:
            continue
        if not abandoned_lease(ctx.std, pool, registry, slot_id, root):
            continue
        lease = registry["slots"][slot_id].get("lease") or {}
        info(ctx.std, "reclaimed the slot held by " + (lease.get("name", "") or "an earlier worktree") +
                      ": its " + label(lease.get("agent") or {}) + " session no longer exists and the checkout was clean")
        registry["slots"][slot_id]["state"] = FREE
        registry["slots"][slot_id]["lease"] = None
        return struct(
            id = slot_id,
            score = 0,
            reused_from = registry["slots"][slot_id].get("last_branch", ""),
        )

    # Nothing scored. Either every owned slot is leased, or a hook refused them
    # all; at the limit the second case has to give way, since the pool is a
    # disk budget and growing it forever is the one outcome worse than a cold
    # build.
    budget = ctx.traits[Worktrees].max_slots
    mine = owned_slots(pool, registry)
    vetoed = _best_free_slot(ctx, pool, registry)
    if len(mine) >= budget and vetoed:
        warn(ctx.std, ("every slot is refused by this repo's score_slot hook and the pool is at " +
                       "its limit of %d; reusing the least-bad slot instead of growing") % budget)
        return struct(id = vetoed, score = 0, reused_from = registry["slots"][vetoed].get("last_branch", ""))

    if len(mine) >= budget:
        # Named in full because the reader is usually an agent, and the useful
        # reply to "the pool is over budget" is the command that fixes it. Not
        # `prune` on its own: at the limit with everything leased there is
        # nothing free for it to take until something is released first.
        warn(ctx.std, ("pool is at its advisory limit of %d slots and all are in use; " +
                       "creating one more checkout and output base. " +
                       "`aspect worktree list` shows what is holding them, " +
                       "`aspect worktree remove <branch>` releases one you are done with, and " +
                       "`aspect worktree prune --max-age-days=<days> --force` then deletes " +
                       "the free ones and their output bases") % budget)

    slot_id = new_slot_id(ctx.std, pool, registry)
    registry["slots"][slot_id] = new_slot_record(pool)
    return struct(id = slot_id, score = 0, reused_from = "")

def _best_free_slot(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, registry: dict) -> str:
    """Any free slot this clone owns, ignoring scoring entirely. The fallback
    for a hook that refused everything at the pool's limit."""
    for slot_id in registry["slots"]:
        slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
        if slot["state"] == FREE and slot.get("owner_git_dir", "") == pool["git_common_dir"]:
            return slot_id
    return ""

# --- putting a worktree in it ------------------------------------------------

def _materialize(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, path: str, name: str, base_ref: str) -> None:
    """Put a git worktree for `name` at the slot, checked out on that branch.

    Whatever the previous occupant left is removed first, so recycling never
    has to care what state the slot was in.

    HEAD is left attached to the branch, which matters more than it looks:
    the branch is the only thing that names a worktree, so a detached HEAD
    leaves `git branch --show-current` empty and the slot unidentifiable — and
    nothing could be committed from it either. Git refuses outright if the
    branch is checked out in another worktree, which is the right answer and
    better than silently detaching.
    """
    if ctx.std.fs.exists(path):
        _git(ctx, ["worktree", "remove", "--force", path], pool["git_common_dir"])
    ctx.std.fs.create_dir_all(pool["dir"])
    existing = _git_try(ctx, ["rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", name], pool["git_common_dir"])
    if existing:
        _git(ctx, ["worktree", "add", path, name], pool["git_common_dir"])
    else:
        # `--no-track` is load-bearing. Without it a branch cut from a
        # remote-tracking ref records that ref as its upstream, and under
        # `push.default=upstream` a later bare `git push` from this worktree
        # targets the base branch — `origin/main` for the common case.
        _git(ctx, ["worktree", "add", "--no-track", "-b", name, path, base_ref], pool["git_common_dir"])

def _check_out_and_lease(
        ctx: TaskContext,
        pool: dict,
        lock: dict,
        registry: dict,
        chosen,
        name: str,
        base_ref: str,
        token: str) -> int | TaskConclusion:
    """Check a worktree out into a slot this caller has reserved, then lease it.

    The pool lock is given back for the checkout and taken again to record the
    result, because the checkout is the whole cost of `add` on a large clone and
    holding the lock through it puts every other agent behind it. The
    reservation is what makes the gap safe: the slot and the branch are both
    claimed for `token`, so no other caller can take either, and a crash in the
    gap leaves a record the next `add` reclaims instead of a slot nothing frees.

    `registry` is re-read afterwards rather than reused. Another caller may have
    changed other slots while the lock was theirs, and writing the copy from
    before would undo that.
    """
    path = slot_path(pool, chosen.id)
    _unlock(ctx, pool, lock)
    err, _ = catch(_materialize, ctx, pool, path, name, base_ref)

    if not _relock(ctx, pool, lock):
        # Nothing can be written safely without the lock, so the reservation
        # stands. This process is about to exit, which is what makes it
        # reclaimable: the next `add` frees the slot and checks the branch out
        # again, onto the same output base.
        if err != None:
            fail("could not create the worktree: " + str(err))
        return _pool_busy(ctx)

    registry = reconcile(ctx.std, pool, read_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"]))

    if err != None:
        _drop_reservation(ctx, pool, registry, chosen.id, token)
        write_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"], registry)
        fail("could not create the worktree: " + str(err))

    if not holds_reservation(registry, chosen.id, token):
        # Only a caller that judged this process dead could have taken the slot,
        # and it is not dead. Leave the registry to whoever owns it now rather
        # than leasing one slot twice, and take the worktree back out so git and
        # the registry still agree.
        catch(run_git, ctx.std, ["worktree", "remove", "--force", path], pool["git_common_dir"])
        warn(ctx.std, "the slot reserved for " + name + " was taken while its worktree was being created")
        return _pool_busy(ctx)

    # Who took it, so `list` can name the session holding each slot rather than
    # only the branch, and so the slot's history says who has had it. Empty for
    # a person at a terminal.
    agent = detect(ctx.std, ctx.args.agent_kind, ctx.args.agent_id)
    record_lease(registry, chosen.id, name, agent)
    slot = registry["slots"][chosen.id]
    slot["state"] = LEASED
    slot["reservation"] = None
    slot["lease"] = {
        "name": name,
        "since": time.now_ms(),
        "agent": agent,
    }
    write_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"], registry)

    return _report_slot(ctx, pool, registry, chosen.id, name, chosen)

# --- reporting ---------------------------------------------------------------

def _checked_out_branches(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict) -> dict:
    """The branch git has in each of this clone's worktrees, by path.

    Git is the authority on what a worktree holds, and a lease records only what
    `add` was asked for. Someone who runs `git switch` inside a slot leaves the
    two disagreeing, and reporting the registry's answer would be reporting the
    stale one.
    """
    porcelain = _git_try(ctx, ["worktree", "list", "--porcelain"], pool["git_common_dir"])
    found = {}
    at = ""
    for line in porcelain.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("worktree "):
            at = line[len("worktree "):].strip()
        elif line.startswith("branch refs/heads/") and at:
            found[at] = line[len("branch refs/heads/"):].strip()
    return found

# What each value in the `state` and `warmth` columns means, as
# `(column, value, definition)`. A listing defines only the values it shows, so
# the common case stays short and a reader never has to leave the terminal to
# learn what `partly warm` buys them. `warmth_kind` is the warmth without its
# pid, which is what makes it matchable here.
# How a slot's registry state reads in the table. Only `add` ever shows
# "setting up", and only for as long as its checkout takes — or until the next
# `add` reclaims it, if that one was killed.
_STATE_LABELS = {LEASED: "in use", FREE: "free", RESERVED: "setting up"}

_COLUMN_NOTES = [
    ("state", "in use", "a worktree is checked out here, leased under the branch shown."),
    ("state", "free", "no worktree here; the slot keeps its Bazel state for whatever comes next."),
    ("state", "setting up", "an `aspect worktree add` is creating a worktree here; the notes say so when the one that started it is gone."),
    ("warmth_kind", "warm", "a live Bazel server still holds this slot's analysis cache, so a build with the same flags skips loading and analysis."),
    ("warmth_kind", "partly warm", "no server, but the output base is there, so `external/` is already materialized."),
    ("warmth_kind", "cold", "no output base yet, so the first build pays for loading, analysis and fetches."),
]

_LIST_FOOTNOTES = [
    "age: how long the slot has existed. leases: how many worktrees it has held, which is how many cold starts it has saved.",
]

_LIST_COLUMNS = [
    Column(key = "branch", header = "branch", always = True),
    Column(key = "state", header = "state", always = True),
    Column(key = "warmth", header = "warmth", always = True),
    Column(key = "idle", header = "idle", right = True),
    # Always: the two numbers that say whether the pool is earning the disk it
    # holds. A reader should not have to pass a flag to see that.
    Column(key = "age", header = "age", right = True, always = True),
    Column(key = "leases", header = "leases", right = True, always = True),
    Column(key = "slot", header = "slot", always = True),
    # Always: a pool with no agents running would otherwise never show that it
    # records which session holds a slot.
    Column(key = "agent", header = "agent", always = True),
    Column(key = "notes", header = "notes"),
]

_HISTORY_COLUMNS = [
    Column(key = "branch", header = "branch", always = True),
    Column(key = "agent", header = "agent", always = True),
    Column(key = "held", header = "held", always = True),
    Column(key = "when", header = "", always = True),
]

def _history_rows(history: list) -> list[dict]:
    """A slot's holders, newest first, as cells for the table renderer.

    Newest first because the recent past is what bears on a build happening
    now; the oldest entry has usually aged out of the slot's memory anyway.
    """
    rows = []
    for entry in reversed(history):
        released = entry.get("released_ms", 0)
        rows.append({
            "branch": entry.get("name", "?"),
            "agent": label(entry.get("agent") or {}),
            "held": duration_phrase(held_for_ms(entry)),
            "when": "still holding it" if not released else "ended %s ago" % duration_phrase(time.now_ms() - released),
        })
    return rows

def _age_phrase(days: int) -> str:
    """How long ago, for a slot's creation date."""
    if days == 0:
        return "created today"
    if days == 1:
        return "created yesterday"
    return "created %d days ago" % days

def _history_note(slot: dict) -> str:
    """How old this slot is and how much use it had before this lease.

    Plain counting, because the pool's whole claim is that a slot gets used
    again: a slot being reused for the seventh time is that claim holding, and a
    pool where every slot reads "new" is one that grew instead of turning over.

    `lease_count` already includes the lease being handed over, so prior use is
    one less — otherwise a slot created a moment ago would report having been
    leased before.
    """
    prior = slot.get("lease_count", 0) - 1
    if prior <= 0:
        return "new slot: this is the first worktree it has held"

    times = "once" if prior == 1 else ("twice" if prior == 2 else "%d times" % prior)
    return "%s, reused %s before this" % (_age_phrase(slot_created_days(slot)), times)

def _warmth_note(std, path: str, reused_from: str, root: str) -> str:
    """One line on what this slot already holds, checked rather than assumed.

    A slot's value is its Bazel state, and that state can be gone — the server
    exits on idle, and `aspect gc` can reclaim the base — so claiming "warm"
    without looking would be a lie often enough to matter.
    """
    warmth = warmth_of(std, path)
    if warmth.server:
        return "warm: a Bazel server is still holding this slot's analysis cache (pid %d)" % warmth.server
    if warmth.base:
        last = (", last used for " + reused_from) if reused_from else ""
        return "partly warm: no server, but this slot's `external/` tree is already materialized" + last
    return "cold: this slot has no Bazel state yet, so the first build pays for it"

# --- task implementations ----------------------------------------------------

def _report_slot(ctx: TaskContext, pool: dict, registry: dict, slot_id: str, name: str, chosen) -> int:
    """Announce a slot the caller now holds, in whichever form it asked for.

    Shared by a fresh lease and a taken-over one, which have to agree on
    `--output=path` and the JSON document or a caller cannot treat them the
    same.
    """
    root = _output_root(ctx)
    path = slot_path(pool, slot_id)
    warmth = warmth_of(ctx.std, path, root)
    if ctx.args.output == "path":
        ctx.std.io.stdout.write(path + "\n")
        return 0
    if ctx.args.output == "json":
        lease = registry["slots"][slot_id].get("lease") or {}
        slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
        ctx.std.io.stdout.write(json.encode({
            "schema_version": _SCHEMA_VERSION,
            "path": path,
            "pool": pool["id"],
            "score": chosen.score,
            "reused_from": chosen.reused_from,
            "agent": lease.get("agent") or {},
            "server_pid": warmth.server,
            "output_base_present": warmth.base,
            "lease_count": slot.get("lease_count", 0),
            "slot_created_days": slot_created_days(slot),
            "output_base": output_base_of(ctx.std, path, root),
        }, indent = 2) + "\n")
        return 0

    info(ctx.std, "worktree ready: " + path)
    print("  branch: " + name)
    print("  " + _warmth_note(ctx.std, path, chosen.reused_from, root))
    print("  " + _history_note(registry["slots"][slot_id]))
    print("")
    print("  slot=$(aspect worktree path " + name + ") && cd \"$slot\"")
    return 0

def _add_impl(ctx: TaskContext) -> int | TaskConclusion:
    name = ctx.args.branch[0]
    cwd = ctx.std.env.current_dir()
    pool = _pool(ctx)
    if not pool:
        return _not_a_repository(ctx)

    # Asked before the lock is taken. None of it writes anything, and the lock
    # exists to serialize the registry, not the questions leading up to it.
    root = _output_root(ctx)
    base_ref = ctx.args.base_ref or _git(ctx, ["rev-parse", "HEAD"], cwd)

    # Score against the tree the slot will actually hold. An existing branch is
    # checked out as itself and `--base-ref` is ignored; only a new branch comes
    # from `base_ref`. Reading the caller's own working tree instead — which is
    # the obvious thing to do and the wrong one — mislabels every `--base-ref`
    # that leaves the current branch, and a dirty lock file mislabels the rest.
    existing = _git_try(ctx, ["rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", name], pool["git_common_dir"])
    checkout_ref = name if existing else base_ref
    want = {
        "name": name,
        "lock_digest": lock_digest_at(ctx.std, pool["git_common_dir"], checkout_ref),
        "commit": _git(ctx, ["rev-parse", checkout_ref], pool["git_common_dir"]),
    }

    lock = _lock_pool(ctx, pool)
    if not lock:
        return _pool_busy(ctx)

    registry = reconcile(ctx.std, pool, read_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"]))

    # Persisted before any early return below. Pruning deletes directories and
    # output bases, so a registry still listing them would hand the next caller
    # a slot whose recorded `lock_digest` describes state that is gone.
    if _age_out(ctx, pool, registry, root):
        write_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"], registry)

    # Before anything is decided, because a slot whose `add` was killed
    # mid-checkout is both unallocatable and holding a branch in git that
    # nothing is going to finish.
    if _reclaim_reservations(ctx, pool, registry):
        write_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"], registry)

    # A branch another `add` is still checking out is taken, even though no
    # lease records it yet.
    pending = reserving_slot(registry, name)
    if pending:
        return _branch_pending(ctx, pool, pending, name)

    # Git would refuse this at checkout anyway — a branch cannot be in two
    # worktrees — but it refuses from inside `_materialize`, after a slot has
    # been picked and its previous occupant force-removed. Both checks here
    # answer before anything is destroyed; the pool's own leases get the better
    # message, so they are tested first.
    held = leased_slot(registry, name)

    # Asking for a branch whose own lease is abandoned is an agent restarting
    # the task it died in. Hand the slot back instead of refusing: the session
    # cannot be resumed and the tree is clean, so there is nothing to protect
    # and the warm base is exactly what the caller wants.
    if held and abandoned_lease(ctx.std, pool, registry, held, root):
        info(ctx.std, "taking back " + name + "'s slot: the session that held it no longer exists")
        free_slot(ctx.std, pool, registry, held)
        write_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"], registry)
        held = ""

    # Taking over is about inheriting a checkout, so it only applies when there
    # is one. A lease pointing at a directory that has been removed behind git's
    # back has nothing to inherit: free it and let ordinary allocation
    # materialize a worktree, rather than reporting a path the caller cannot
    # enter.
    if held and ctx.args.take_over:
        if ctx.std.fs.exists(slot_path(pool, held)):
            _take_over(ctx, pool, registry, held, name)
            write_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"], registry)
            return _report_slot(ctx, pool, registry, held, name, struct(score = 0, reused_from = name))
        free_slot(ctx.std, pool, registry, held)
        write_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"], registry)
        held = ""

    if held:
        return _refuse(ctx, "branch_in_use", _held_by(ctx, pool, registry, held, name))
    elsewhere = _worktree_holding_branch(ctx, pool, name)
    if elsewhere:
        return _refuse(
            ctx,
            "branch_elsewhere",
            name + " is already checked out at " + elsewhere +
            ", which this pool does not manage. Work there, or move that " +
            "worktree off the branch first.",
        )

    chosen = _pick_slot(ctx, pool, registry, want, root)
    token = reserve_slot(ctx.std, registry, chosen.id, name)
    write_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"], registry)
    return _check_out_and_lease(ctx, pool, lock, registry, chosen, name, base_ref, token)

def _remove_impl(ctx: TaskContext) -> int | TaskConclusion:
    name = ctx.args.name[0]
    pool = _pool(ctx)
    if not pool:
        return _not_a_repository(ctx)
    if not _lock_pool(ctx, pool):
        return _pool_busy(ctx)

    root = _output_root(ctx)
    registry = reconcile(ctx.std, pool, read_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"]))
    target = leased_slot(registry, name)
    if not target:
        return _no_such_worktree(ctx, name)

    slot = registry["slots"][target]
    path = slot_path(pool, target)

    # Releasing drops the worktree, which takes anything uncommitted in it with
    # it. That is the last step of an ordinary task, so it must not be the step
    # that loses work: name the files and let the caller decide.
    dirty = _dirty_files(ctx, path, root)
    if dirty and not ctx.args.force:
        return _refuse(
            ctx,
            "worktree_dirty",
            name + " has uncommitted changes at " + path + ":\n" + dirty +
            "\nCommit or stash them, or pass --force to discard them with the worktree.",
        )

    # What the slot is left holding is what the next allocation scores it on, so
    # a slot whose checkout has vanished must record nothing rather than keep an
    # earlier occupant's digest — which would score it 100 for state it does not
    # have and send the next caller to the coldest slot in the pool.
    head = ""
    if ctx.std.fs.exists(path):
        head = _git(ctx, ["rev-parse", "HEAD"], path)
        slot["lock_digest"] = lock_digest(ctx.std, path)
        dropped = run_git(ctx.std, ["worktree", "remove", "--force", path], pool["git_common_dir"])
        if not dropped.ok:
            # Freeing the slot anyway is right — the next `add` force-removes
            # whatever is in its way — but a silent failure leaves `list`
            # claiming the slot is empty while git still holds a worktree there.
            warn(ctx.std, "could not drop the worktree at " + path + ": " + dropped.err +
                          "; the slot is free and the next allocation will clear it")
    else:
        slot["lock_digest"] = ""

    close_lease(registry, target)
    slot["state"] = FREE
    slot["last_commit"] = head
    slot["last_branch"] = name
    slot["lease"] = None
    write_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"], registry)

    if ctx.args.output == "json":
        ctx.std.io.stdout.write(json.encode({
            "schema_version": _SCHEMA_VERSION,
            "released": name,
            "path": path,
        }, indent = 2) + "\n")
        return 0
    info(ctx.std, "released " + name + "; the slot stays in the pool, " +
                  _warmth_note(ctx.std, path, name, root).partition(":")[0])
    return 0

def _path_impl(ctx: TaskContext) -> int | TaskConclusion:
    name = ctx.args.name[0]
    pool = _pool(ctx)
    if not pool:
        return _not_a_repository(ctx)
    registry = reconcile(ctx.std, pool, read_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"]))
    found = leased_slot(registry, name)
    if not found:
        return _no_such_worktree(ctx, name)

    path = slot_path(pool, found)
    if ctx.args.output == "json":
        ctx.std.io.stdout.write(json.encode({
            "schema_version": _SCHEMA_VERSION,
            "path": path,
            "pool": pool["id"],
        }, indent = 2) + "\n")
        return 0
    ctx.std.io.stdout.write(path + "\n")
    return 0

def _prune_impl(ctx: TaskContext) -> int | TaskConclusion:
    pool = _pool(ctx)
    if not pool:
        return _not_a_repository(ctx)
    if not _lock_pool(ctx, pool):
        return _pool_busy(ctx)

    root = _output_root(ctx)
    registry = reconcile(ctx.std, pool, read_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"]))
    victims = prunable_slots(ctx.std, pool, registry, ctx.args.max_age_days, root)

    # The whole list goes out before anything is deleted, so the confirmation is
    # answered with the picture rather than a count.
    candidates = []
    if victims:
        info(ctx.std, "%d slot(s) free and unused for %d+ days:" % (len(victims), ctx.args.max_age_days))
    for slot_id in victims:
        slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
        label = slot.get("last_branch", "") or "never used"
        candidates.append({
            "path": slot_path(pool, slot_id),
            "idle_days": slot_age_days(ctx.std, pool, slot_id, slot, root),
            "last_branch": slot.get("last_branch", ""),
        })
        print("    %s  %d days  %s" % (slot_path(pool, slot_id), slot_age_days(ctx.std, pool, slot_id, slot, root), label))

    doomed = "delete %d worktree slot(s) and their Bazel output bases" % len(victims)
    verdict = confirm.decide(
        has_work = len(victims) > 0,
        dry_run = ctx.args.dry_run,
        force = ctx.args.force,
        is_tty = ctx.std.io.stdin.is_tty,
    )
    if verdict == confirm.NEEDS_FORCE:
        return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 1, message = confirm.needs_force_message(doomed))
    question = "Delete %d worktree slot(s) and their Bazel output bases?" % len(victims)
    if verdict == confirm.ASK and not confirm.ask(ctx, question):
        return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 0, message = "left alone; nothing was deleted.")

    pruned = []
    kept = []
    if verdict != confirm.SKIP:
        for i, slot_id in enumerate(victims):
            path = slot_path(pool, slot_id)
            print("  [%d/%d] removing %s" % (i + 1, len(victims), path))
            outcome = drop_slot(ctx.std, pool, registry, slot_id, root)
            if outcome.dropped:
                pruned.append(path)
            else:
                kept.append((path, outcome.reason))
        write_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"], registry)

    for path, reason in kept:
        warn(ctx.std, "kept " + path + ": " + reason)

    if ctx.args.output == "json":
        ctx.std.io.stdout.write(json.encode({
            "schema_version": _SCHEMA_VERSION,
            "pool": pool["id"],
            "dry_run": ctx.args.dry_run,
            "max_age_days": ctx.args.max_age_days,
            # Candidates are reported whether or not anything was deleted, so a
            # dry run says what it would do rather than only that it did
            # nothing.
            "candidates": candidates,
            "pruned": pruned,
            "kept": [{"path": p, "reason": r} for p, r in kept],
        }, indent = 2) + "\n")
        return 0

    if not victims:
        info(ctx.std, "nothing to prune: no free slot has been idle for %d days" % ctx.args.max_age_days)
    elif verdict == confirm.SKIP:
        info(ctx.std, "dry run; nothing was deleted")
    else:
        info(ctx.std, "pruned %d slot(s)" % len(pruned))
    return 0

def _list_impl(ctx: TaskContext) -> int | TaskConclusion:
    # No lock: this only reads. `write_registry` renames into place, so a
    # concurrent `add` can never be observed half-written, and reconciling
    # without persisting costs nothing — `add` and `remove` reconcile too.
    pool = _pool(ctx)
    if not pool:
        return _not_a_repository(ctx)
    root = _output_root(ctx)
    registry = reconcile(ctx.std, pool, read_registry(ctx.std, pool["dir"]))
    checked_out = _checked_out_branches(ctx, pool)
    rows = []
    for slot_id in registry["slots"]:
        slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
        lease = slot.get("lease") or {}
        path = slot_path(pool, slot_id)
        warmth = warmth_of(ctx.std, path, root)
        agent = lease.get("agent") or {}
        rows.append({
            "slot": slot_id,
            "state": slot["state"],
            "name": lease.get("name", ""),
            # The branch an in-flight `add` is checking out here. Separate from
            # `name`, which is the lease `path` and `remove` answer to and does
            # not exist yet.
            "reserved_for": (slot.get("reservation") or {}).get("name", ""),
            # What git has, which is the authority; `name` is only what `add`
            # recorded and `path`/`remove` still look up by.
            "branch": checked_out.get(path, ""),
            "agent": agent,
            "session_state": session_state(ctx.std, agent) if agent else "",
            "path": path,
            "last_branch": slot.get("last_branch", ""),
            "owner": slot.get("owner_git_dir", ""),
            "owned_here": slot.get("owner_git_dir", "") == pool["git_common_dir"],
            "idle_days": slot_age_days(ctx.std, pool, slot_id, slot, root),
            "created_days": slot_created_days(slot),
            "lease_count": slot.get("lease_count", 0),
            "history": slot.get("history") or [],
            "output_base": output_base_of(ctx.std, path, root),
            "server_pid": warmth.server,
            "output_base_present": warmth.base,
        })

    if ctx.args.output == "json":
        ctx.std.io.stdout.write(json.encode({
            "schema_version": _SCHEMA_VERSION,
            "pool": pool["id"],
            "remote": pool["remote"],
            "slots": rows,
        }, indent = 2) + "\n")
        return 0

    info(ctx.std, "pool " + pool["id"])
    if not rows:
        print("  no slots yet — `aspect worktree add <branch>` creates the first")
        return 0

    foreign = 0
    resumable = []
    cells = []
    for r in rows:
        if r["server_pid"]:
            kind, warmth = "warm", "warm (server %d)" % r["server_pid"]
        elif r["output_base_present"]:
            kind, warmth = "partly warm", "partly warm"
        else:
            kind, warmth = "cold", "cold"

        notes = []
        if not r["owned_here"]:
            foreign += 1
            notes.append("other clone")
        if r["state"] == RESERVED:
            if r["reserved_for"] and r["reserved_for"] != r["branch"]:
                notes.append("being set up for " + r["reserved_for"])
            if reservation_abandoned(ctx.std, registry, r["slot"]):
                notes.append("that add is no longer running; the next one reclaims this slot")

        # Only session states a reader can act on are noted. `unknown` is the
        # normal answer for a harness whose session layout cannot be checked, so
        # printing it would put noise on most rows and say nothing.
        if r["session_state"] == RESUMABLE or r["session_state"] == GONE:
            notes.append("session " + r["session_state"])
        if r["session_state"] == RESUMABLE:
            hint = resume_hint(r["agent"])
            if hint:
                resumable.append((r["name"], hint))

        # The column shows what git has. When that is not what the lease was
        # taken under, the lease name is still the handle `path` and `remove`
        # answer to, so it has to be said.
        if r["branch"] and r["name"] and r["branch"] != r["name"]:
            notes.append("leased as " + r["name"])

        cells.append({
            "branch": r["branch"] or r["name"] or r["reserved_for"] or r["last_branch"] or "-",
            "state": _STATE_LABELS.get(r["state"], r["state"]),
            "warmth": warmth,
            "warmth_kind": kind,
            # Idle days are what `prune` acts on, so a reader deciding whether to
            # run it sees the same number the decision uses. Only a free slot has
            # a meaningful age, and only a free slot can be pruned.
            "idle": "%dd" % r["idle_days"] if r["state"] == FREE else "",
            "age": "%dd" % r["created_days"],
            "leases": str(r["lease_count"]),
            "slot": r["slot"],
            "agent": label(r["agent"]),
            "notes": ", ".join(notes),
        })

    for line in render(cells, _LIST_COLUMNS):
        print(line)
    print("")
    for line in legend(cells, _COLUMN_NOTES):
        print(line)
    for note in _LIST_FOOTNOTES:
        print("  " + note)

    # The paths behind each row. Warmth says whether a base is there; only this
    # says which one, which is what you need when one goes missing.
    if ctx.args.verbose:
        print("")
        for r in rows:
            print("  " + (r["branch"] or r["name"] or r["reserved_for"] or r["last_branch"] or r["slot"]))
            print("    slot:        " + r["path"])
            print("    output base: " + r["output_base"] +
                  ("" if r["output_base_present"] else "  (missing)"))
            print("    leases:      %d, %s" % (r["lease_count"], _age_phrase(r["created_days"])))
            for line in render(_history_rows(r["history"]), _HISTORY_COLUMNS):
                print("    " + line)
    print("")
    if foreign:
        print("  %d slot(s) belong to another clone of this repository and cannot be leased here." % foreign)

    # A session whose process has exited is almost always a closed terminal, so
    # the useful offer is how to get back into it, not how to take it away.
    for name, hint in resumable:
        print("  %s is held by a session that is not running. Resume it:" % name)
        print("    " + hint)
    print("  slot=$(aspect worktree path <branch>) && cd \"$slot\"  to enter one")
    print("  aspect worktree prune --dry-run  to see what has gone stale")
    return 0

# --- task definitions --------------------------------------------------------

_OUTPUT_ARG = {
    "output": args.string(
        default = "text",
        values = ["text", "json"],
        description = "stdout format. `text` (default) writes a human-readable report to stderr and leaves stdout empty. `json` writes one document to stdout.",
    ),
}

# `add` additionally offers `path`, so one command both creates a worktree and
# tells a shell where to go: cd "$(aspect worktree add fix/login --output=path)"
_ADD_OUTPUT_ARG = {
    "output": args.string(
        default = "text",
        values = ["text", "json", "path"],
        description = "stdout format. `text` (default) writes a human-readable report to stderr and leaves stdout empty. `json` writes one document to stdout. `path` writes only the worktree directory, so a shell can step into it: `slot=$(aspect worktree add <branch> --output=path) && cd \"$slot\"`. Check the status — `cd \"$(...)\"` returns 0 even when the command fails, because `cd \"\"` is a no-op.",
    ),
}

add = task(
    group = ["worktree"],
    summary = "Take a git worktree from a pool of reused paths, so Bazel starts warm.",
    description = "Create a git worktree at a pooled slot, checked out on the named branch. The slot's path never changes, so the Bazel server bound to it survives being handed from one task to the next — the second and every later worktree in that slot starts with a warm server and an `external/` tree already materialized.\n\nPrefer this over `git worktree add`, which gives Bazel a path it has never seen and therefore a cold server every time.\n\nThe slot path is printed, and `aspect worktree path <branch>` prints it again later; `git worktree list` reports it too, so the worktree stays legible to git and to anyone managing it by hand.",
    implementation = _add_impl,
    traits = [Worktrees],
    args = {
        "branch": args.positional(
            minimum = 1,
            maximum = 1,
            description = "Branch to check out. Created from --base-ref when it does not exist yet.",
        ),
        "base_ref": args.string(
            default = "",
            description = "Ref to branch from when creating a new branch. Defaults to the current HEAD.",
        ),
        "agent_kind": args.string(
            default = "",
            description = "Name of the agent harness taking this worktree, recorded on the lease and shown by `aspect worktree list`. Detected automatically for harnesses the CLI recognizes, or set `ASPECT_AGENT_KIND`; pass this when neither applies.",
        ),
        "agent_id": args.string(
            default = "",
            description = "Session identifier of the agent taking this worktree, recorded on the lease so a slot can be traced back to the session holding it. Detected automatically where the harness exports one, or set `ASPECT_AGENT_ID`.",
        ),
        "take_over": args.boolean(
            default = False,
            description = "Claim a slot this pool already leases for the branch, re-assigning it to this session. The checkout is left exactly as it is, so anything the previous session had uncommitted is still there. Refused without this flag, because the other session may only be suspended and resumable.",
        ),
    } | _ADD_OUTPUT_ARG,
)

remove = task(
    group = ["worktree"],
    summary = "Release a worktree and return its slot to the pool, Bazel state intact.",
    description = "Remove the worktree and return the slot to the pool. The slot's path is what Bazel keys its server and output base on, so leaving that path in place is what makes the next lease of this slot fast.\n\nSkipping this never breaks anything: git is the authority on whether a worktree exists, so a slot whose worktree git has forgotten is free regardless of what the pool recorded. A slot whose checkout is still on disk stays leased, though, and is taken back only once the session that holds it is gone and the checkout is clean — so call this when you can.",
    implementation = _remove_impl,
    traits = [Worktrees],
    args = {
        "name": args.positional(
            minimum = 1,
            maximum = 1,
            description = "The name the worktree was created with.",
        ),
        "force": args.boolean(
            default = False,
            description = "Release the slot even when the worktree has uncommitted changes, discarding them. Without this, a dirty worktree is refused and the changed files are listed.",
        ),
    } | _OUTPUT_ARG,
)

path = task(
    kind = "path",
    group = ["worktree"],
    summary = "Print a leased worktree's directory, for `cd`.",
    description = "Print the slot directory a leased worktree lives in, and nothing else, so a shell can step into it:\n\n    slot=$(aspect worktree path fix/login) && cd \"$slot\"\n\nThe two steps matter: `cd \"$(...)\"` returns 0 even when this fails, because the substitution is empty and `cd \"\"` is a no-op, so a script would carry on where it already was.\n\nA task cannot change its caller's directory, so this prints rather than moves. Define a shell function if you want one word:\n\n    aspect-cd() { local d; d=$(aspect worktree path \"$1\") && cd \"$d\"; }",
    implementation = _path_impl,
    traits = [Worktrees],
    args = {
        "name": args.positional(
            minimum = 1,
            maximum = 1,
            description = "The name the worktree was created with.",
        ),
    } | _OUTPUT_ARG,
)

# `list` would shadow the builtin type within this module, so the symbol is
# named for its command and `kind` carries the command name.
prune = task(
    group = ["worktree"],
    summary = "Delete pooled slots that have gone stale, and their Bazel output bases.",
    description = "Reclaim the disk held by free slots nothing has used for a while. Each slot costs a checkout plus a Bazel output base, so a pool that grew during a busy week keeps paying for it afterwards.\n\nOnly free slots are considered — a slot someone is working in is never deleted, whatever its age — and a slot whose Bazel server is still running is reported and kept, since unlinking an output base from under a live server is how a build starts writing into directories that no longer exist.\n\nSlots stranded by a clone that was moved or deleted are collected too: no clone can lease them again, because `git worktree add` is scoped to one object store.\n\nThis also runs automatically during `aspect worktree add`, so the pool stays bounded without anyone remembering to clean up.",
    implementation = _prune_impl,
    traits = [Worktrees],
    args = {
        "max_age_days": args.int(
            default = PRUNE_AGE_DAYS,
            description = "Delete free slots untouched for at least this many days. `0` considers every free slot.",
        ),
        "dry_run": args.boolean(
            default = False,
            description = "Report what would be deleted and delete nothing.",
        ),
        "force": args.boolean(
            default = False,
            description = "Delete without asking. Required when stdin is not a terminal, so a script or an agent has to opt in rather than have a prompt answered for it.",
        ),
    } | _OUTPUT_ARG,
)

worktree_list = task(
    kind = "list",
    group = ["worktree"],
    summary = "List this repository's worktree slots, what holds them, and how warm each is.",
    description = "Show every slot in this repository's pool: which are leased and to what, and which are free along with the branch each last held. A free slot's last branch is the signal for how warm it will be for the next thing you put in it.",
    implementation = _list_impl,
    traits = [Worktrees],
    args = {
        "verbose": args.boolean(
            default = False,
            description = "Also print each slot's directory and the Bazel output base its path derives to, marked when that base is missing.",
        ),
    } | _OUTPUT_ARG,
)

# Exposed for worktree_test.axl; not part of any task's surface.
testonly_held_by = _held_by
testonly_list_columns = _LIST_COLUMNS
